# Target C-II — repeated TB1 inactive witnesses collapse to an ultra-active row-parallel family

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the one-sign / `NEAR-ACTIVE` side of the strong-TB1 contact-swap branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** TB1 inverse-`D` cone collapse; contact-minimal overswap; exact path-layer support; source unit separation and diameter bound  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_double_witness_collapse_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The contact-swap branch previously used only one fact about inactive witnesses: a fixed strict pair can be moved by at most two disjoint path-layer translations. That gave the factor-two loss from one-sign TB1 interfaces to distinct inverse-`D` witnesses.

The common-axis theorem makes the multiplicity-two case much more rigid.

If the **same** previously strict pair blocks the overswap at two distinct one-sign TB1 interfaces, then the two layer incidences orient that pair in opposite directions, while the two overswap axes differ by only `O(D^-1)`. The pair can therefore hit the same active boundary twice only if its original slack is one order smaller than the generic contact-swap window:

\[
\boxed{
|xy|-1=O(D^{-2})
\quad\text{or}\quad
D-|xy|=O(D^{-2}).
}
\]

Moreover its projective direction is `O(D^-1)` from the **common row line**. Thus a repeated inactive witness is not an arbitrary inverse-`D` obstruction. It is an ultra-near-active pair aligned with the global train phase.

This has a further consequence in the near-diameter case. A family of chords of length `D-O(D^-2)` whose projective directions all lie in one `O(D^-1)` row cone has **bounded degree**, independent of `D`. Hence any linear family of repeated near-diameter witnesses contains a linear vertex-disjoint matching. The previous generic near-diameter witness theorem only gave an `Omega(D^(1/4))` matching because it had no common direction control.

At the current strong-TB1 density floor

\[
s_0={791\over320{,}000{,}000},
\]

let `M_D` be the one-sign population, so on the `NEAR-ACTIVE` side

\[
|M_D|>{s_0\over2}D-o(D).
\]

Choose one contact-minimal overswap witness at every interface. Then either

\[
\boxed{
>{3s_0\over8}D-o(D)
=(9.26953125\times10^{-7})D-o(D)
}
\]

distinct witness pairs occur, improving the previous unconditional `s_0/4` distinct-witness floor, or at least

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over8}D-o(D)
=(3.08984375\times10^{-7})D-o(D)
}
\]

distinct pairs are each selected by **two** interfaces and therefore belong to the ultra-active row-parallel class above.

After splitting the repeated family by lower/upper active boundary, one type has density at least

\[
{s_0\over16}=1.544921875\times10^{-7}.
\]

- repeated near-unit witnesses contain a vertex-disjoint matching of size
  \[
  \boxed{
  >{s_0\over176}D-o(D)
  =(1.4044744318\ldots\times10^{-8})D-o(D);
  }
  \]
- repeated near-diameter witnesses also contain `Omega(D)` vertex-disjoint pairs, now because their degree is bounded by a source-independent constant.

So the old multiplicity-two bookkeeping loss has a geometric price: if it is used on a positive-density set, it creates a linear matching of ultra-near-active pairs locked to the common row direction.

---

## 1. Uniform overswap notation

At a one-sign strong-TB1 interface `i`, let

\[
q_i=|a_i+b_i|,
\qquad
w_i={a_i+b_i\over q_i},
\]

be the exact-swap length and midpoint opening direction. The contact-minimal overswap uses

\[
\lambda_D=1+D^{-1},
\qquad
v_i=-s_iw_i,
\qquad
s_i:=\lambda_Dq_i.
\tag{DW.1}
\]

The cone-collapse theorem gives, uniformly over the strong-TB1 population,

\[
s_i=O(D^{-1})
\tag{DW.2}
\]

and one common oriented transverse direction `w_*` such that

\[
|w_i-w_*|=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{DW.3}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
d_{ij}:=|w_i-w_j|=O(D^{-1})
}
\tag{DW.4}
\]

uniformly for every two strong-TB1 interfaces `i,j`.

The constants in these `O` terms are absolute in the current high-`t` branch. In particular, the explicit cone theorem has `q_i<(31.4\times10^6)D^{-1}+o(D^{-1})`; no lower bound on `q_i` is needed below.

For an oriented source pair `h=xy`, write

\[
r=p_y-p_x,
\qquad \ell=|r|.
\]

At layer `R_i`, define its orientation-independent inward vector

\[
n_i:=\delta_i r,
\qquad
\delta_i=\mathbf1_{R_i}(y)-\mathbf1_{R_i}(x)\in\{-1,0,1\}.
\tag{DW.5}
\]

If the pair moves at interface `i`, then `|n_i|=ell` and its squared distance after the overswap is exactly

\[
\boxed{
\ell_i'^2
=\ell^2-2s_i(n_i\cdot w_i)+s_i^2.
}
\tag{DW.6}

---

## 2. A pair moved by two layers has opposite inward orientations

The physical path layers `R_i` are pairwise disjoint. If one unordered pair `xy` moves under two distinct layers `R_i,R_j`, then one endpoint belongs to `R_i` and the other belongs to `R_j`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{n_j=-n_i.}
\tag{DW.7}
\]

This is stronger than the earlier multiplicity-two count: the two possible layer tags act on the same original segment with exactly opposite incidence signs.

Fix such a pair and put

\[
n:=n_i=-n_j.
\]

The rest of the argument uses only (DW.4), (DW.6), and this sign reversal.

---

## 3. Double near-unit witnesses have `O(D^-2)` slack

Assume the same previously strict pair is a lower-bound witness for both overswaps. Thus

\[
\ell>1,
\qquad
\ell_i'\le1,
\qquad
\ell_j'\le1.
\tag{DW.8}
\]

Put

\[
A:=\ell^2-1>0.
\]

Using `n_i=n` in (DW.6), the first witness inequality gives

\[
n\cdot w_i
\ge {A+s_i^2\over2s_i}
={A\over2s_i}+{s_i\over2}.
\tag{DW.9}
\]

Using `n_j=-n`, the second gives

\[
n\cdot w_j
\le -{A+s_j^2\over2s_j}
=-{A\over2s_j}-{s_j\over2}.
\tag{DW.10}
\]

Subtracting,

\[
n\cdot(w_i-w_j)
\ge
{A\over2}\left({1\over s_i}+{1\over s_j}\right)
+{s_i+s_j\over2}.
\tag{DW.11}
\]

Let

\[
H_{ij}:={s_is_j\over s_i+s_j}.
\]

Since the left side of (DW.11) is at most `ell d_ij`, multiplication by `2H_ij` gives the exact necessary bound

\[
\boxed{
A+s_is_j
\le2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij}.
}
\tag{DW.12}

Now

\[
H_{ij}=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad
d_{ij}=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad\ell=1+O(D^{-1})
\]

for every near-unit contact-swap witness. Hence

\[
A=O(D^{-2}).
\]

Since

\[
\ell-1={A\over\ell+1},
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{
\ell-1=O(D^{-2}).
}
\tag{DW.13}

Thus a pair cannot be reused as a near-unit overswap blocker at two interfaces while retaining generic `Theta(D^-1)` lower slack.

There is also directional information. Equations (DW.9)--(DW.10) imply

\[
0<n\cdot w_i<\ell d_{ij},
\qquad
-\ell d_{ij}<n\cdot w_j<0.
\tag{DW.14}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\left|{n\over\ell}\cdot w_i\right|,
\left|{n\over\ell}\cdot w_j\right|
=O(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{DW.15}

Together with (DW.3), the projective direction of `xy` is `O(D^-1)` from the common row line `[u_*]=[J^{-1}w_*]`.

---

## 4. Double near-diameter witnesses also have `O(D^-2)` slack

Now assume the same pair is an upper-bound witness for both overswaps:

\[
\ell<D,
\qquad
\ell_i'\ge D,
\qquad
\ell_j'\ge D.
\tag{DW.16}
\]

Put

\[
B:=D^2-\ell^2>0.
\]

The first overswap inequality gives

\[
n\cdot w_i
\le {s_i\over2}-{B\over2s_i},
\tag{DW.17}
\]

while the second, using `n_j=-n`, gives

\[
n\cdot w_j
\ge {B\over2s_j}-{s_j\over2}.
\tag{DW.18}
\]

Hence

\[
n\cdot(w_j-w_i)
\ge
{B\over2}\left({1\over s_i}+{1\over s_j}\right)
-{s_i+s_j\over2}.
\tag{DW.19}
\]

Again bounding the left side by `ell d_ij` and multiplying by `2H_ij` yields

\[
\boxed{
B-s_is_j
\le2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij},
}
\tag{DW.20}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
B
\le2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij}+s_is_j.
}
\tag{DW.21}

Here

\[
\ell=D-O(D^{-1}),
\qquad d_{ij}=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad H_{ij}=O(D^{-1}),
\]

so

\[
B=O(D^{-1}).
\]

Since

\[
D-\ell={B\over D+\ell},
\]

we get the stronger original-length window

\[
\boxed{
D-\ell=O(D^{-2}).
}
\tag{DW.22}

The direction again collapses to the common row line. From (DW.17),

\[
n\cdot w_i\le{s_i\over2},
\]

and from (DW.18) plus `|n\cdot(w_i-w_j)|<=ell d_ij`,

\[
n\cdot w_i
\ge-{s_j\over2}-\ell d_{ij}.
\]

Thus

\[
\left|{n\over\ell}\cdot w_i\right|
\le d_{ij}+{s_i+s_j\over2\ell}
=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{DW.23}
\]

The same holds with `i,j` exchanged, and (DW.3) gives

\[
\boxed{
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([xy],[u_*])=O(D^{-1}).}
\tag{DW.24}

So a reused upper-slack witness is simultaneously ultra-near-diametrical and locked to the global row phase.

---

## 5. The same pair cannot switch witness type

For large `D`, the two contact-swap windows

\[
(1,1+O(D^{-1})]
\]

and

\[
[D-O(D^{-1}),D)
\]

are disjoint. The witness type is determined by the original length `ell`, not by the interface.

Therefore if one strict pair is selected as the overswap witness at two interfaces, it is covered by exactly one of Sections 3 or 4. Every repeated selected witness is consequently an `O(D^-2)`-slack, `O(D^-1)` row-parallel pair.

---

## 6. Repeated near-diameter witnesses have bounded degree

The row-direction conclusion changes the matching scale for the repeated near-diameter family.

### Lemma DW.1 — bounded degree in a narrow near-diameter cone

Fix constants `A,L>0`. For all sufficiently large `D`, let `G_D(A,L)` be the graph on a unit-separated diameter-`D` source whose edges satisfy

\[
D-{A\over D^2}\le|xy|<D
\tag{DW.25}
\]

and

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([xy],[u_*])\le{L\over D}.
\tag{DW.26}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{\Delta(G_D(A,L))=O_{A,L}(1).}
\tag{DW.27}

### Proof

Fix a vertex `x`. For an incident edge `xy`, orient the ray from `x` to `y`. Projective closeness in (DW.26) gives two possible oriented clusters, one within `L/D` of `+u_*` and one within `L/D` of `-u_*`.

Both clusters cannot occur at the same vertex. If `y` lies in the positive cluster and `z` in the negative cluster, then with

\[
r=|x-y|,\qquad s=|x-z|,
\]

we have `r,s>=D-A/D^2`, and the `u_*`-projection of `y-z` has magnitude at least

\[
(r+s)\cos(L/D)
\ge2\left(D-{A\over D^2}\right)\cos(L/D)
>D
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`. This contradicts the source diameter bound.

Thus all incident witness rays lie in one oriented cone of width at most `2L/D`. For two neighbors `y,z` in that cone,

\[
||x-y|-|x-z||\le {A\over D^2}
\]

and their unit ray directions differ by at most `2L/D`. Hence

\[
|y-z|
\le {A\over D^2}+2D\sin(L/D)
\le2L+1
\tag{DW.28}
\]

for all large `D`.

So all neighbors of `x` lie in a fixed-radius disk around any one of them. Unit separation bounds the number of source points in such a disk by a constant depending only on `L` (for example, disjoint radius-`1/2` disks give a crude area bound). This proves (DW.27). `square`

Because the constants in (DW.22)--(DW.24) are uniform over the strong-TB1 population, all repeated near-diameter witnesses lie in one graph `G_D(A,L)` for fixed sufficiently large `A,L`. Their maximum degree is therefore bounded independently of `D`.

A greedy matching consequently loses only `O(1)` witness edges per retained edge. Thus

\[
\boxed{
\Theta(D)\text{ repeated near-diameter witnesses}
\Longrightarrow
\Theta(D)\text{ vertex-disjoint repeated near-diameter witnesses}.
}
\tag{DW.29}

This is the main quantitative gain over the generic inactive near-diameter theorem.

---

## 7. Global density dichotomy for the one-sign TB1 branch

On the `NEAR-ACTIVE` side, let

\[
M:=|M_D|>{s_0\over2}D-o(D).
\tag{DW.30}

Choose one overswap witness for each one-sign interface. By the exact layer-support theorem, each unordered pair can be selected at most twice.

Let

- `d` be the number of distinct selected pairs used twice;
- `s` be the number of distinct selected pairs used once.

Then exactly

\[
M=s+2d,
\qquad
N_{\rm distinct}=s+d=M-d.
\tag{DW.31}

There are two cases.

### Case A — few repeated selected witnesses

If

\[
d<{M\over4},
\]

then

\[
N_{\rm distinct}=M-d>{3M\over4}.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm distinct}
>{3s_0\over8}D-o(D)
=(9.26953125\times10^{-7})D-o(D).
}
\tag{DW.32}

This improves the earlier factor-two distinct-witness floor on this subcase.

### Case B — many repeated selected witnesses

If

\[
d\ge{M\over4},
\]

then Sections 3--5 give at least

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over8}D-o(D)
=(3.08984375\times10^{-7})D-o(D)
}
\tag{DW.33}

**distinct repeated** witness pairs, every one satisfying

\[
\operatorname{dist}(|xy|,\{1,D\})=O(D^{-2})
\tag{DW.34}
\]

and

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([xy],[u_*])=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{DW.35}

Split these pairs into near-unit and near-diameter types. One type has size

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over16}D-o(D)
=(1.544921875\times10^{-7})D-o(D).
}
\tag{DW.36}

If the near-unit type wins, the degree-six almost-contact theorem from the inactive-slack matching note gives a greedy matching of size

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over176}D-o(D)
=(1.4044744318\ldots\times10^{-8})D-o(D).
}
\tag{DW.37}

If the near-diameter type wins, Lemma DW.1 gives a source-independent degree bound and hence

\[
\boxed{
\Omega(D)
}
\tag{DW.38}

vertex-disjoint repeated near-diameter pairs. All of them have `O(D^-2)` diameter slack and the same inverse-`D` projective row cone.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The inactive-slack side of strong TB1 now has a sharper support normal form:

```text
one-sign strong-TB1 population of linear density
+
contact-minimal O(1/D) overswap at every interface
+
all overswap axes in one O(1/D) transverse cone

-> either

   > (3 s0 / 8) D distinct inverse-D inactive witnesses,
   so witness selection is nearly injective at the interface scale;

or

   > (s0 / 8) D repeated witnesses,
   each simultaneously
      O(D^-2) from the unit or diameter boundary
      and O(D^-1) from the common row projective direction;

   after fixing the active boundary,
   a linear vertex-disjoint ultra-active matching survives.
```

This does not close C-II. It does remove the main ambiguity in the previous factor-two witness count. Reusing one inactive pair at two layer swaps is possible only inside a much thinner geometric exceptional set, and that exceptional set itself has linear matching structure.

The next TB1 target can now split cleanly:

1. on the near-injective side, try a simultaneous family of separated overswaps, because most chosen blockers are distinct at the interface scale;
2. on the repeated side, compare the linear `O(D^-2)`-slack row-parallel matching directly with the common near-equilateral train rectangle. In the near-diameter subcase the projective cone is now narrow enough to give bounded degree, not merely the previous `O(D^(3/4))` degree.

**Disposition:** upgraded multiplicity-two bookkeeping into a geometric collapse theorem. A pair reused as a TB1 inactive witness is forced one order closer to activation and into the common row direction; a positive-density repeated near-diameter family has a linear matching. C-II and Level I remain open.